,title,link,text,language,family
0,Music of Papua New Guinea - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Papua_New_Guinea,"December 18, 2025 - 1949 saw the first Papuan to achieve international fame, Blasius To Una, begin his career. By the end of the 1970s, a local recording industry had appeared and artists such as Sanguma and later George Telek began mixing native and Western styles like rock and jazz.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
1,Meet The Papuan Youth Group Reviving Their Heritage | Samdhana.org,https://samdhana.org/stories/meet-papuan-youth-group-reviving-their-heritage,"March 5, 2022 - The event also featured a forum that explored current issues, hopes, aspirations and future plans. This group discussion was led by Torianus Kalami, an activitist from Gerakan Muda Suku Moi (the Moi Tribal Youth Movement). Participants then enjoyed a wide range of music performances, dances, poetry readings and sharing of folklore.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
2,The Spirit of Mambesak West Papuan Freedom Music - The Commons,https://commonslibrary.org/the-spirit-of-mambesak-west-papuan-freedom-music/,"February 15, 2026 - Other Papuan pop bands have recorded patriotic songs that challenged the Indonesian control of a country that for decades they referred to as Irian Jaya – Trio Ambisi’s Tanah Papua being another example, or Edo Konodologit’s Aku Papua. In the late 80’s, an art-rock band from Melbourne called Not Drowning, Waving arrived in Papua New Guinea. They were interested initially in the music of the country, but while there they encountered West Papuan refugees fleeing violence and living in the Blackwater refugee camp near the border.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
3,Music - Free West Papua Campaign,https://www.freewestpapua.org/resources/music/,"April 2, 2026 - There have been a number of albums released in recent years in support of the Free West Papua movement. These span from music recorded by West Papuans (including Mambesak and the Lani Singers), through to compilation releases featuring artists supportive of the cause.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
4,"Moi, Mosana in Indonesia people group profile | Joshua Project",https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19079/ID,"The Moi are sometimes known as the Mosana, Mooi, or Mekwei people, and they prefer to be called Mekwei. Their language is Moi Kelim.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
5,"West Papua Music Collection | Reggae from nabire, ID",https://www.reverbnation.com/westpapuamusiccollection,"Reggae music, lyrics, and videos from nabire, ID on ReverbNation",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
6,"Moi Kelim Language (MXN) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/mxn/,Moi Kelim is an endangered indigenous language of Indonesia. It belongs to the West Papuan language family. The language is used as a first language by adults only. It is not known to be taught in schools.,Moi Kelim,West Papuan
7,Moi language,https://grokipedia.com/page/moi_language,"The Moi language is a West Papuan language spoken primarily by the Moi ethnic group in the western Bird's Head Peninsula of Southwest Papua, Indonesia, encompassing two closely related but distinct varieties: Moi Kelim (ISO 639-3: mxn) and Moi Lemas (ISO 639-3: sbg).[1] Classified within the West Bird's Head subgroup of the West Papuan phylum, it features low lexical similarity with neighboring languages (e.g., 44% between its varieties, 33% with Kalabra), and is characterized by tonal distinctions, such as the slower, lower-toned speech of Moi Lemas compared to the higher-pitched Moi Kelim.[2",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
8,Southwest Papua — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Southwest_Papua,"January 17, 2026 - Arfak tumbutana dances, performed with rhythmic movements and chants, serve as oral traditions imparting character values like politeness during greetings or feasts, often accompanied by pig sacrifices for celebrations.[208][209] Moi dances, distinct to subgroups like Kelim, feature stylized steps mimicking forest spirits, enacted at initiations or to welcome allies, preserving motifs tied to marine totems.[210] Housing reflects environmental adaptation: Arfak build elevated mod aki aksa (thousand-legged houses) on stilts up to 2-3 meters high using hardwood and rattan, designed for flood-pron",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
9,"People, Culture and Nature: Preserving Papuan Heritage, Together | Samdhana.org",https://www.samdhana.org/stories/people-culture-and-nature-preserving-papuan-heritage-together,"Yoram Kalami is a member of SALSES. He insists the younger generation of the Moi Kelim are committed to conserving their natural heritage, pointing to a series of customary regulations designed to prevent and punish illegal practices in the forest. “Shooting birds is strictly prohibited,” he says, highlighting the harsh fines and penalties handed out to offenders.",Moi Kelim,West Papuan
